ratory information system  rule that we built  so they could come in and get an  appointment so uh for retesting so that  was a nice outcome of of  implementing this this cut point  um and so we actually did implement that  6.0 cut point we also implemented the  cut point  of greater than equal to 5.3 on all of  our hospital labs for that fasting  sample that's done on site  we didn't have to worry about you know a  rc curve analysis because we are testing  plasma glucose  and so we say okay if the fasting result  is 5.3 or more then it's a diagnosis and  we don't need to do any  further testing so the patient can go  home  so this was a very nice um result at the  end of the day so  by these two changes we save about 145  women per year  from having to get the glucose drink so  this is a great um a great  outcome and it saves the lab about 10  000 a year  so this has been published in the may  2019 edition of the journal of applied  laboratory medicine so you can  read more about it there now  the second quality improvement study  using data  i'd like to show you is a project we  embarked on regarding  72-hour fecal fat testing so this test  is a very old test  it came to be you know four years after  the end of the second world war um  it's a it's a manual test using large  quantities of acids bases  and uh and solvents and  it requires a patient to go sort of on a  fat treadmill they consume a high fat  diet for a total of five days in the  last three  days of that diet they collect their  stool in a paint can  and the whole purpose of this test is to  identify fat male absorption and male  digestion  in children you know the high fat diet  is  is is recommended but not expected and  so  dietary intake is measured with a  dietary assessment tool and fat intake  is calculated based upon that  and compared to the fat output that we  measure in the 72-hour collection  so this is what it looks like so you  start with a stool specimen in a paint  can and then you dilute it in water  and then you add rocks to the can and  shake it up in a paint shaker  to make a slurry and then you extract  the stool fats which are mostly in  triglyceride format  at that stage in ethanol and you add to  it a very strong base and warm it and  that creates soaps  it turns the um breaks down the  triglycerides into  into fatty acids and glycerol and then  converts the triglycerides into fatty  acid soaps  and fatty acids into fatty acid soaps  and glycerol  then um you add hydrochloric acid to  convert the soaps back into fatty acids  you protonate them  you do it on ice because it's an  exothermic reaction and then you extract  those fatty acids with petroleum  ether while shaking vigorously  and then you evaporate the petroleum  ether in a hot water bath leaving fatty  acid crystals behind  which you solubilize in ethanol  then you add to it a ph indicator and  titrate it with a strong base  until you get a nice blue color and the  amount of base that you titrate in  millimoles  is proportional to the moles of fatty  acids present in that aliquot  which then you can use to back calculate  the total amount of fatty acid  in the original 72 hour stool specimen  and then you can express that result  in millimoles excreted per day now  we have two or we did have two reference  intervals for this test  for patients under 7 years of age what  was considered to be normal was  less than 7 millimoles per day output  but for patients greater than equal to 7  years of age it was less than 21  moles per day output we had a separate  reference interval  um when comparing fat intake versus fat  output as a percent  these are for those children that are  assessed by a dietary assessment tool a  diet diary  and that was zero to 9.9 but most of our  testing  um our reporting was the millimoles per  day  so some interesting information  historically on the metrics  uh performance on this test um so our  turnaround time for this test was two  weeks  from acetic specimentary reporting  result um and we eventually moved to one  month turnaround time when we made some  changes to it  and it was a very labor-intensive test  as i've mentioned  but it took one staff member an entire  shift to report out nine results which  is really low output  um the test had 20 variation in the  results of month to month  in our quality control pools and our run  to run variation was 10  in specimens that we would run and carry  over from from  the previous run to the current run so  the maximum total and precision of this  test was 22  not very good when you compare it to  automated chemistry analysis  uh other other interesting things about  this test is we were not evaluated by  any  agency for our performance relative to  our peers just because one did not exist  there was no proficiency tests in the  scheme um  so impacts of this test while it's  difficult for patients to  collect their stool for three days but  also difficult to go into a high fat  diet for five days  they also write down their diet on a  piece of paper regardless of whether a  child or an adult and for the adults  that  information was not used for the kids  the diet has to be recorded by the  family for those days  and stool collection in a for a child is  rather difficult  um you know i suppose it's easier if  they're in diapers but it's still  not easy and it's hard for staff there's  significant bio safety controls chemical  hygiene controls  it's a manual procedure and then if we  are doing  intake versus output input versus output  in children we have to have a dietitian  involved to make sure that the total fat  intake is calculated properly from the  diet data  lots of problems in terms of  interpretation so we'd often get  incomplete um collections um any  patients um who  actually were experiencing steatorrhea  um their response to that would be  reduce their fat  intake to make their life a little bit  more pleasant diarrhea itself  less unless is a lesser known 