atistic or area under the  curve is 0.74 so that's actually quite  high  for something as simple as the weight of  the specimen  as a predictor of abnormality and if you  go along this curve and you look at  different cut points  say 486 grams 555 606 grams  um we start off very sensitive but not  very specific and as we increase we get  more specific and less sensitive that's  of course what happens  but the point of this is that we had  options of setting a cut point  based upon a chosen sensitivity and  specificity  and that was that could be chosen in  consultation with clinicians  similar results if we just looked at the  under seven year of age  group with seven millimoles per day  excretion  so we had multiple different cut points  with appropriate sensitivity  specificities that we could choose  um by using weight alone and then when  we  actually looked at some of the specimens  that we banked for these patients and  and  tested them for the presence of fat  globules microscopically  we found that adding microscopic  assessment for fat globules on top  of fecal weight in terms of predicting  positivity by the vana camera test  it improved the prediction it went from  0.70 to 0.74  for airing to the curve so that was  quite satisfying  and so um everyone was agreement that we  could replace the bandit camera test  with an assessment of weight  and assessment of fat lobules and just  put out this  big comment which says um the following  about the specimen that the weight is  and we enter the result the technology  enters the result and decides whether  it's high or normal  and it indicates the upper limit is  normal for that test and the appropriate  sensitivity and specificity  and then some further information about  black law bills and to contact myself if  there's any problems  so this also has been published this was  published in may of last year  and you can read about in the journal of  applied laboratory medicine  so the final six minutes of my talk i'll  use to  describe my final application of  laboratory data  in quality improvement so this was  actually using common laboratory results  to predict  risk of death in patients undergoing  coronary catheterization  so um you know what's interesting about  acute coronary syndrome patients is that  they can be discharged from the hospital  but then die later  days or weeks after they have their  initial catheterization  and here's some statistics the risk of  death 30 days after catherine  after initial acs episode is four  percent but by six months it's eight  percent  and if they're readmitted for um for a  cardiac cause  their their risk of death goes up  enormously  and so basically the idea is that you  want to keep patients in the hospital  initially that need it  and discharge only those patients who  are going to stay healthy or stay  healthy for as long as possible  if they come back to the hospital  there's higher more bit more morbidity  mortality and there's a greater impact  on the healthcare system  and in canada we know that up to 60 of  these unnecessary readmissions  may be preventable by being more careful  in how we assess the patients while  they're in hospital  now one solution from the lab is  actually to use existing laboratory data  to identify those who are at risk and of  course  you know the question is are we missing  gold in our backyard that gold being  simple stuff that's already in the  electronic medical record that results  in very simple laboratory tests  and so this is a big area investigation  by a number of  hospital systems in the u.s and also in  different  locations within canada as well so our  objective was to use  common lab data we're talking cbc  electrolytes to estimate  risk of death in these patients and  contrast that risk  and how well the risk is assigned to the  patients the efficiency of it  to the assessment that's used  traditionally  you know for these patients so  assessment of clinical risk factors  smoking status presence of comorbidities  body mass index family history  previous mi these sorts of things and  that'll tell us whether  that risk assessment via lab data is  useful or not so all we did  is we pulled all available laboratory  test data on patients undergoing  coronary catheterization and we linked  it  to a cardiac registry that had follow-up  data and mortality  and comorbidity that we contrast the lab  data to  and then we determine the associations  of lab test results with mortality  and created a cumulative lab risk score  that summarizes the cumulative impact of  all the different lab results on  mortality risk  and then we validated that lab risk  score in terms of its performance in a  different geographic location to be sure  that it actually works  um so this is a theoretical graph of the  um you know  uh the the course of admission and  discharge for a patient  who comes in with an acute coronary  syndrome they're admitted  they have labs they may have other labs  they have other assessments  on the left once they hit the red box  then they're having  their final set of labs and examinations  before an invasive procedure  coronary catheterization stenting  and then perhaps after the procedure  there's post-procedure labs  other investigations followed by labs  before discharge  and you know then there would be an  outcome  occurring at any point during that  process but hopefully long after they've  been discharged  the simple lab tests that we considered  in our analysis which is the cbc with  differential  electrolytes creatinine as calculated by  the  egfr total co2 and  inr and we put these data  into a logistic regression model the  data being  quintiles of each of the different lab  variables  and we essentially used a backwards  elimination technique to kick out  variables  that were not significantly associated  with mortality risk  in the linked data set after adjusting  for the impact  of the other lab lab data and so  what ended up