the meter and i'll get into  exactly what that means later  the glucose drink's not given to the  patient  um then the whole blood if the if the um  well at that point the whole blood  sample is spun down  and prepared for transport and if the  initial glucose result was adequate then  um  the glucose drink is given and post load  samples are taken from the patient  and then those samples are spun down and  then all the samples are sent to our  central  um testing lab um in the north  northwestern quadrant of the city  and what's being sent is plasma so this  sample is effectively stabilized at that  point  now to get the testing done it takes  about four hours um from dispatch of the  specimen to the result being populated  in the electronic medical record  now at our urban and rural hospitals uh  we don't have um  the exact same process instead specimens  are drawn spun  and plasma testing is done on site so  you get an immediate result  um on the fasting sample and so if the  result is considered to be too high  then the drink is not given and that  that process is done immediately  and then any subsequent testing of the  specimens is also done immediately  so we get an immediate result and the  patient can walk out  and know that the results are are going  into that medical record  so just some review on what we define as  diabetes  so for non-pregnant patients we define  diabetes according to diabetes  association guidelines which are the  same as  american diabetes except that we use si  units so  diabetes is defined as fasting glucose  greater than equal to seven  and a one c of greater than equal to six  point five percent  or a two hour um post load 75 gram low  glucose of  upgraded middle or equal to 11.1 um you  can also diagnose it based on a random  glucose of greater equal 11.1  but any positive of any of these results  should be followed up with confirmation  by another test if the patient is  asymptomatic  well for pregnant women when we're  trying to diagnose gestational diabetes  we use the two-step um procedure  that starts with a 50 gram load in the  center  box 50 gram glucose load with one hour  post glucose testing  and depending on their glucose result  after that they can be  negative on the far left swim lane they  could be positive on the far right or  they can be intermediate between 7.8 and  11.  if that happens then they are referred  for a 75 gram glucose tolerance test  where we test post low glucose at one  hour  and two hours but before all of that we  actually do a fasting glucose  and if a fasting glucose or a one hour  post low glucose or a two hour post low  glucose any of them  if they are elevated then gestational  diabetes is diagnosed so i want to draw  your attention  to the fasting glucose greater than or  equal to 5.3  so even before any postflow glucose  testing can be done  we can diagnose gestational diabetes in  these patients  so back to the drink why we  why should the drink not be given well  i've alluded to that  um but first of all um you know it's  actually quite inconvenient for the  patient to have to  consume the drink and then wait around  for two hours on subsequent blood draws  to figure out if they have gestational  diabetes or not  or or just regular diabetes if they're  non-pregnant there's also a certain  amount of discomfort and risk associated  with tolerance testing  there's blood collection that's involved  in pregnant women particularly they're  susceptible to vomiting and nausea  these are the major causes for  cancelling these tests  and there is a small risk of  hyperglycemia or or  post load hypoglycemia due to  overactive beta cells and and a  hyperinsulinemic response  and there's some costs associated with  the lab as well which we're very  cognizant of  being part of a public health care  system in canada there's costs and  consumables there's cost to do the  testing  and there's actual staff being involved  in the testing which prevents  other patients from being seen too  um and the patient occupies a chair at  the collection site which could be  taken by another patient um so uh  what is considered to be too high on  that glucose meter at those  collection sites well for the  non-pregnant tolerant tests  we basically have a cutoff of 7.9  millimoles per liter or greater  um and you know if that is exceeded then  we don't get the drink  and we came up with that because it's  7.0 plus  0.8 and the 0.8 is the  whole blood meter versus plasma core lab  bias that we see  and the 7.0 is the threshold for  diabetes but for the pregnant tolerance  tests  we don't we at the point of this project  didn't have a cut off for  not for denying the drink so our  objective was to define a meter cup  point with  adequate sensitivity and specificity for  gestational diabetes by fasting plasma  alone  and the fasting plasma result alone  would be greater than equal to 5.3  millivolts per liter  so we can make a call of whether patient  has gestational diabetes  right away if we can implement that cut  point  so our methods were first of all to  obtain all of our whole blood fasting  glucose media results  we link it to the plasma fasting glucose  result from the same  tube of blood remember we're testing  them on both the meteor and the lab  and then perform rock curve analysis to  identify a  fasting whole blood meter cut point that  has high sensitivity and specificity  for that fasting plasma glucose of  greater than equal to 5.3  you know the meter cannot be used to  diagnose gestational diabetes  but we can if we set a threshold high  enough  we can probably predict whether  someone's going to go on and get a  plasma result of 5.3 or greater  so this is just a bland altman plot  showing the relationship between  the core laboratory plots and glucose on  the bottom and  the difference between the two on on the  on the y  axis and all it's showing is that you  know our glucose meters are  overestimating um  uh plasma glucose on the low end of the  lab result  so i'm not going to show you the gory  details of the rock curve analysis but  at the bottom highlighted in yellow  is the cut point of 5.2 which is  actually greater than or equal to 5.3  and that cut point on the meter has a  sensitivity of 91.4  and specificity of 91 for  a plasma glucose of greater than equal  to 5.3  and so over a two year period we  end up identifying um 323  women who actually have a plasma glucose  over  over or or equal to 5.3 using that meter  cut point so that's great  so those women can be they don't have to  have the drink at all so that's an  improvement  in their experience going to the  collection site  but there's 370 individuals who are  denied the drink inappropriately and  those women are going to have to come  back for retesting because that cut  point  uh is not the best uh based upon the  number of women that we test  um and so there's another cut point that  we looked at too and that's 6.0  so it only has a sensitivity to 25 but a  specificity of 99.9  and that one we called  88 individuals over two years  if we if we use that cut point who would  be appropriately denied the glucose  drink  and over that period of time there are  only three individuals  who we inappropriately denied it to if  we would have implemented it  so this was you know all this data is  showing what we would get  if we implemented these cut points is  based on our existing data so we looked  at this data and thought to ourselves  what should we  put into place here and so we had to  talk to the endocrinologist to discuss  with them  what would be best and we all agreed  that using a cut point of greater than  equal to  6.1 or greater than equal to 6 was  better  because there were only three false  denials of the drink per year  and you know basically individuals that  had a you know an error  in this process meaning they would be  denied the drink inappropriately  they would get a automatic callback  through a laboratory information system  rule that we built  so they could come in and get an  appointment so uh for retesting so that  was a nice outcome of of  implementing this this cut point  um and so we actually did implement that  6.0 cut point we also implemented the  cut point  of greater than equal to 5.3 on all of  our hospital labs for that fasting  sample that's done on site  we didn't have to worry about you know a  rc curve analysis because we are testing  plasma glucose  and so we say okay if the fasting result  is 5.3 or more then it's a diagnosis and  we don't need to do any  further testing so the patient can go  home  so this was a very nice um result at the  end of the day so  by these two changes we save about 145  women per year  from having to get the glucose drink so  this is a great um a great  outcome and it saves the lab about 10  000 a year  so this has been published in the may  2019 edition of the journal of applied  laboratory medicine so you can  read more about it there now  the second quality improvement study  using data  i'd like to show you is a project we  embarked on regarding  72-hour fecal fat testing so this test  is a very old test  it came to be you know four years after  the end of the second world war um  it's a it's a manual test using large  quantities of acids bases  and uh and solvents and  it requires a patient to go sort of on a  fat treadmill they consume a high fat  diet for a total of five days in the  last three  days of that diet they collect their  stool in a paint can  and the whole purpose of this test is to  identify fat male absorption and male  digestion  in children you know the high fat diet  is  is is recommended but not expected and  so  dietary intake is measured with a  dietary assessment tool and fat intake  is calculated based upon that  and compared to the fat output that we  measure in the 72-hour collection  so this is what it looks like so you  start with a stool specimen in a paint  can and then you dilute it in water  and then you add rocks to the can and  shake it up in a paint shaker  to make a slurry and then you extract  the stool fats which are mostly in  triglyceride format  at that stage in ethanol and you add to  it a very strong base and warm it and  that creates soaps  it turns the um breaks down the  triglycerides into  into fatty acids and glycerol and then  converts the triglycerides into fatty  acid soaps  and fatty acids into fatty acid soaps  and glycerol  then um you add hydrochloric acid to  convert the soaps back into fatty acids  you protonate them  you do it on ice because it's an  exothermic reaction and then you extract  those fatty acids with petroleum  ether while shaking vigorously  and then you evaporate the petroleum  ether in a hot water bath leaving fatty  acid crystals behind  which you solubilize in ethanol  then you add to it a ph indicator and  titrate it with a strong base  until you get a nice blue color and the  amount of base that you titrate in  millimoles  is proportional to the moles of fatty  acids present in that aliquot  which then you can use to back calculate  the total amount of fatty acid  in the original 72 hour stool specimen  and then you can express that result  in millimoles excreted per day now  we have two or we did have two reference  intervals for this test  for patients under 7 years of age what  was considered to be normal was  less than 7 millimoles per day output  but for patients greater than equal to 7  years of age it was less than 21  moles per day output we had a separate  reference interval  um when comparing fat intake versus fat  output as a percent  these are for those children that are  assessed by a dietary assessment tool a  diet diary  and that was zero to 9.9 but most of our  testing  um our reporting was the millimoles per  day  so some interesting information  historically on the metrics  uh performance on this test um so our  turnaround time for this test was two  weeks  from acetic specimentary reporting  result um and we eventually moved to one  month turnaround time when we made some  changes to it  and it was a very labor-intensive test  as i've mentioned  but it took one staff member an entire  shift to report out nine results which  is really low output  um the test had 20 variation in the  results of month to month  in our quality control pools and our run  to run variation was 10  in specimens that we would run and carry  over from from  the previous run to the current run so  the maximum total and precision of this  test was 22  not very good when you compare it to  automated chemistry analysis  uh other other interesting things about  this test is we were not evaluated by  any  agency for our performance relative to  our peers just because one did not exist  there was no proficiency tests in the  scheme um  so impacts of this test while it's  difficult for patients to  collect their stool for three days but  also difficult to go into a high fat  diet for five days  they also write down their diet on a  piece of paper regardless of whether a  child or an adult and for the adults  that  information was not used for the kids  the diet has to be recorded by the  family for those days  and stool collection in a for a child is  rather difficult  um you know i suppose it's easier if  they're in diapers but it's still  not easy and it's hard for staff there's  significant bio safety controls chemical  hygiene controls  it's a manual procedure and then if we  are doing  intake versus output input versus output  in children we have to have a dietitian  involved to make sure that the total fat  intake is calculated properly from the  diet data  lots of problems in terms of  interpretation so we'd often get  incomplete um collections um any  patients um who  actually were experiencing steatorrhea  um their response to that would be  reduce their fat  intake to make their life a little bit  more pleasant diarrhea itself  less unless is a lesser known fact  actually increases fat output no matter  the actual pathology that causes it  and then the final step the titration  step  the variation associated with that  measure was actually  surprisingly large and most of this  information was summarized in an audit  that was done in the late 90s and  published in early 2000 and the  bottom line from that audit um that  british audit was that  this test makes it makes it a very  unsatisfactory investigation and the  performance of it  certainly and probably of little  clinical value so  um when we were looking at this um you  know  this test was identified uh as one that  could be  probably not done so this is about 15  years after this came out  so there's lots of alternatives to this  test that's another point of this  article  i'm not going to get into too many of  them here but basically we have other  tests in the lab that can be used that  are simpler  that can help rule in or rule out fat  male absorption and maldigestion  there are some patients that you just  don't want to do this test on you know  they're probably not eating enough to  have  you know sufficient fat intake to begin  with  you know patients with frank pancreatic  insufficiency it's not really  useful to do this test um and then if  you if you're just trying to  diagnose it and the patient has gray and  greasy stool i don't think you need to  do any other testing  and save maybe confirming fat lobules  that are present in the specimen  so our goal was to first of all reduce  the amount of fecal fat testing done in  our lab and hopefully replace the test  because it's a really bad test and not  that helpful for  clinical investigations so what we did  was we first of all  pulled all of our utilization data for  fecal fat um  we looked at ordering patterns to  determine if we can um  you know immediately institute some  restrictions for a quick win  and also we performed some research on  the existing data  in the lab information system to see if  any characteristics of the specimen can  predict abnormality  by the van der camer test and we also  did some testing of bank specimens to  see if that  those results from alternative tests  would help  so first thing we found out was that you  know in these bar graphs um the majority  of the  of the tests that were being run were on  patients who were adults greater than  equal to 18 years of age  and that's inappropriate this test was  really designed to pick up  you know extreme fat malnutrition in  kids so we instituted a  ordering restriction before 2015 and  that reduced  our ordering of this test and our  testing of this test by 80  right off the bat um so that was that  was a very quick win but we still had  some residual ordering  um in kids and adults um and so  um we decided that you know this test  could only be done by permission  by consultation with myself and any  specimens that arrived at the lab  that that were not pre-approved by  myself they would be screened with a  microscopic investigation for fat  globules  um and we found that um you know when  presented with these  paws and egg results the clinicians  admitted that they probably didn't need  to do any further testing  um and sometimes um we would uh well  in a later stage of the intervention we  just started to run  this test reflexively and reporting the  results  and um you know the the physicians never  complained  um and when asked about it they said  well we probably never needed the fecal  fat  camera test originally so this is a good  move  um in terms of the data how we use it we  looked at whether the weight of the  specimen  could predict abnormality by the fecal  fat test itself so this is an roc curve  analysis  of the specimen weight the 72-hour  specimen weight versus positivity  um using the 21 millimole per per day  um cut off uh for patients that are  greater than equal to seven years of age  and the first thing you see um you know  is it's not a bad roc curve  and the c statistic or area under the  curve is 0.74 so that's actually quite  high  for something as simple as the weight of  the specimen  as a predictor of abnormality and if you  go along this curve and you look at  different cut points  say 486 grams 555 606 grams  um we start off very sensitive but not  very specific and as we increase we get  more specific and less sensitive that's  of course what happens  but the point of this is that we had  options of setting a cut point  based upon a chosen sensitivity and  specificity  and that was that could be chosen in  consultation with clinicians  similar results if we just looked at the  under seven year of age  group with seven millimoles per day  excretion  so we had multiple different cut points  with appropriate sensitivity  specificities that we could choose  um by using weight alone and then when  we  actually looked at some of the specimens  that we banked for these patients and  and  tested them for the presence of fat  globules microscopically  we found that adding microscopic  assessment for fat globules on top  of fecal weight in terms of predicting  positivity by the vana camera test  it improved the prediction it went from  0.70 to 0.74  for airing to the curve so that was  quite satisfying  and so um everyone was agreement that we  could replace the bandit camera test  with an assessment of weight  and assessment of fat lobules and just  put out this  big comment which says um the following  about the specimen that the weight is  and we enter the result the technology  enters the result and decides whether  it's high or normal  and it indicates the upper limit is  normal for that test and the appropriate  sensitivity and specificity  and then some further information about  black law bills and to contact myself if  there's any problems  so this also has been published this was  published in may of last year  and you can read about in the journal of  applied laboratory medicine  so the final six minutes of my talk i'll  use to  describe my final application of  laboratory data  in quality improvement so this was  actually using common laboratory results  to predict  risk of death in patients undergoing  coronary catheterization  so um you know what's interesting about  acute coronary syndrome patients is that  they can be discharged from the hospital  but then die later  days or weeks after they have their  initial catheterization  and here's some statistics the risk of  death 30 days after catherine  after initial acs episode is four  percent but by six months it's eight  percent  and if they're readmitted for um for a  cardiac cause  their their risk of death goes up  enormously  and so basically the idea is that you  want to keep patients in the hospital  initially that need it  and discharge only those patients who  are going to stay healthy or stay  healthy for as long as possible  if they come back to the hospital  there's higher more bit more morbidity  mortality and there's a greater impact  on the healthcare system  and in canada we know that up to 60 of  these unnecessary readmissions  may be preventable by being more careful  in how we assess the patients while  they're in hospital  now one solution from the lab is  actually to use existing laboratory data  to identify those who are at risk and of  course  you know the question is are we missing  gold in our backyard that gold being  simple stuff that's already in the  electronic medical record that results  in very simple laboratory tests  and so this is a big area investigation  by a number of  hospital systems in the u.s and also in  different  locations within canada as well so our  objective was to use  common lab data we're talking cbc  electrolytes to estimate  risk of death in these patients and  contrast that risk  and how well the risk is assigned to the  patients the efficiency of it  to the assessment that's used  traditionally  you know for these patients so  assessment of clinical risk factors  smoking status presence of comorbidities  body mass index family history  previous mi these sorts of things and  that'll tell us whether  that risk assessment via lab data is  useful or not so all we did  is we pulled all available laboratory  test data on patients undergoing  coronary catheterization and we linked  it  to a cardiac registry that had follow-up  data and mortality  and comorbidity that we contrast the lab  data to  and then we determine the associations  of lab test results with mortality  and created a cumulative lab risk score  that summarizes the cumulative impact of  all the different lab results on  mortality risk  and then we validated that lab risk  score in terms of its performance in a  different geographic location to be sure  that it actually works  um so this is a theoretical graph of the  um you know  uh the the course of admission and  discharge for a patient  who comes in with an acute coronary  syndrome they're admitted  they have labs they may have other labs  they have other assessments  on the left once they hit the red box  then they're having  their final set of labs and examinations  before an invasive procedure  coronary catheterization stenting  and then perhaps after the procedure  there's post-procedure labs  other investigations followed by labs  before discharge  and you know then there would be an  outcome  occurring at any point during that  process but hopefully long after they've  been discharged  the simple lab tests that we considered  in our analysis which is the cbc with  differential  electrolytes creatinine as calculated by  the  egfr total co2 and  inr and we put these data  into a logistic regression model the  data being  quintiles of each of the different lab  variables  and we essentially used a backwards  elimination technique to kick out  variables  that were not significantly associated  with mortality risk  in the linked data set after adjusting  for the impact  of the other lab lab data and so  what ended up in the final model were  things like sex  co2 sodium egfr white  white blood cell count and inr most of  these are  obviously quite important in the  assessment of patients but their  associations as indicated by the odds  ratios  some of them were unexpected for example  the higher the sodium was  the lower the risk universally for  mortality for the patient and that may  reflect  a medical response while they're while  they're an inpatient  or it may just be the provision of  fluids and and more intense  medical care to the patient um and so  what we did  with those associations in the final  model is we  created a cumulative score um a lab risk  score and you know  this logistic regression model it's  basically just a formula that adds  the individual contributions of each  predictor age sex and all the lab  variables  to create an overall risk of death for  the patient  and if you multiply those coefficients  by a constant  to make the score large and easy to work  with you can get a final number for that  patient  maybe 0.5 and if we divide those scores  into quintiles  you can graph them like this and you see  as you increase the quintile  the lab square score you increase their  risk of mortality quite substantially  unadjusted there's a 40 times higher  risk of death in these patients in the  top quintile versus the bottom  and what we found is that the c  statistic the overall performance  calcification performance of the score  in calgary in the validation population  um was very good and almost as good as  the demographic and clinical risk  factors  and this performance was was almost the  same in edmonton our valve our  external validation location  so lab data is powerful and this paper  was published  also in the journal of applied  laboratory medicine um last july  so our overall conclusions is that you  know first of all  quality improvement requires good data  which we have access to in calgary  um you have to be able to work with data  very well in order to get a conclusion  but it's really fun and extremely  rewarding so  thank you for your attention i hope you  found this talk interesting
 

